@startuml delegated-action-handler
!theme plain
' Updated: accepted architecture-review findings (2026-10-13)
title Delegated action (v1.3): an external orchestrator commands a registered action under a grant chain; the gate verifies the chain, the registry names the handler, which is a deterministic process (B) or an agent (A)

participant "External orchestrator\n(other owner's user, key signed by G2)" as XO
participant "Their key keeper" as XK
database "Shared room\n(federated carrier)" as MX
participant "Our carrier provider" as MP
participant "Our bridge\n(trust gate, decision guard)" as BR
participant "Trust registry\n(keys, grants, actions)" as T
participant "Handler A: admin orchestrator\n(OS user admin_orch)" as HA
participant "Handler B: runner helper\n(privileged process)" as HB
participant "Our session host" as SH

note over XO, SH
  Illustrative route only; unused delegated effects stay closed (TRU §4).
  Before the first consumer, its contract package must publish a
  constructible typed action/target path preserving exact signed proof.
  ADM session-envelope grammar is not extended here; peer data gains no authority.
end note

== issue (before) ==
note over T
  G1: owner root -> external owner key K_alexis:
      actions github-runner.*, target host:mini/runners/*,
      delegable depth 1, expiry
  G2: K_alexis -> their orchestrator key: subset of G1
  registry: namespace github-runner.* -> handler (A or B), schema
end note

== command ==
XO -> XK: sign envelope {action github-runner.restart,\ntarget host:mini/runners/x, grant_refs [G2, G1], expiry, nonce}
XK --> XO: signature
XO -> MX: post envelope (metadata carries the signature)
MX -> MP: event
MP -> MP: persist (fsync), then advance cursor
MP -> BR: envelope bytes
BR -> T: canonical bytes; key_id pinned? signature ok?\nchain G2 -> G1 -> our root: each link signed by its issuer,\nsubject == next issuer, actions/targets narrow, expiries nested;\nrevocation snapshot; freshness; replay; target binding
alt any check fails
  T --> BR: reject{reason}
  BR -> MP: receipt refused; audit
  MP -> MX: receipt
else chain valid
  BR -> BR: decision guard: request fits the grants and the schema;\nverification record {chain, checks, fingerprint}
  BR -> T: handler for github-runner.* on host:mini?
  alt handler is an agent (A)
    T --> BR: handler = admin_orch
    BR -> SH: enqueue(action item, authoritative, verification_ref) for admin_orch
    SH -> HA: stdin: validated command <id>, fetch from mailbox
    HA -> BR: mailbox read <id> → body + verification record
    HA -> HA: optional re-verification; judgement (safe to restart now?)
    HA -> HB: execute through the helper or its own narrow sudo rule
    HB --> HA: done
    HA -> BR: signed receipt applied | refused{reason}
  else handler is a process (B)
    T --> BR: handler = runner helper (socket)
    BR -> HB: admitted command + verification record
    HB -> T: re-verify the chain itself (never trusts the bridge alone)
    HB -> HB: one registered operation: restart runner x; audit line
    HB -> BR: receipt applied | refused{reason}, signed by the helper's identity
  end
  BR -> MP: receipt (signed)
  MP -> MX: receipt in the thread
  MX -> XO: receipt visible to the external orchestrator
end

note over BR, HB
  No free text in the authority layer: the action is a registered
  name with a schema; the target is concrete; wildcards exist only
  in grants. The requester never judges its own request.
end note
@enduml
